{{Jainism}}

[[File:12th-century Yogasastra, Hemachandra Jainism, Sanskrit, 1 mm Devanagari script.jpg|thumb|right|upright=1.25|A 12th-century manuscript of [[Hemachandra]]'s ''[[Yogaśāstra]]'' in Sanskrit. The text is notable for using 1&nbsp;mm miniaturized Devanagari script.]]
'''Jain literature''' ({{langx|sa|जैन साहित्य}}) refers to the literature of the [[Jainism|Jain religion]]. It is a vast and ancient literary tradition, which was initially transmitted orally. The oldest surviving material is contained in the canonical ''Jain Agamas'', which are written in [[Ardhamagadhi Prakrit|Ardhamagadhi]], a [[Prakrit]] ([[Middle Indo-Aryan languages|Middle Indo-Aryan]]) language. Various commentaries were written on these canonical texts by later [[Jain monasticism|Jain monks]]. Later works were also written in other languages, such as Sanskrit and [[Maharashtri Prakrit]].

Jain literature is primarily divided between the canons of the ''[[Digambara]]'' and ''[[Śvētāmbara]]'' orders. These two main sects of Jainism do not always agree on which texts should be considered authoritative.

More recent Jain literature has also been written in other languages, such as [[Marathi language|Marathi]], [[Tamil language|Tamil]], [[Rajasthani language|Rajasthani]], [[Dhundari language|Dhundari]], [[Marwari language|Marwari]], [[Hindi language|Hindi]], [[Gujarati language|Gujarati]], [[Kannada]], [[Malayalam]], and, more recently, in [[English language|English]].

{{Short description|Branch of biology concerning depressive disorders in humans}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=September 2020}}
The '''biology of depression''' is the attempt to identify a biochemical origin of [[Depression (mood)|depression]], as opposed to theories that emphasize psychological or situational causes.<ref>{{citation |title=The Etiology of Depression |date=2009 |work=Depression in Parents, Parenting, and Children: Opportunities to Improve Identification, Treatment, and Prevention |url=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK215119/ |access-date=2 March 2025 |publisher=National Academies Press (US) |last1=England |first1=Mary Jane |last2=Sim |first2=Leslie J.}}</ref><!-- This sentence is copied onto [[Depression (mood)]]; consider reflecting any changes made here onto that article. -->

Scientific studies have found that different brain areas show altered activity in humans with [[major depressive disorder|major depressive disorder (MDD)]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Zhang |first1=Fei-Fei |last2=Peng |first2=Wei |last3=Sweeney |first3=John A. |last4=Jia |first4=Zhi-Yun |last5=Gong |first5=Qi-Yong |date=November 2018 |title=Brain structure alterations in depression: Psychoradiological evidence |journal=CNS Neuroscience & Therapeutics |volume=24 |issue=11 |pages=994–1003 |doi=10.1111/cns.12835 |issn=1755-5949 |pmc=6489983 |pmid=29508560}}</ref> Further, nutritional deficiencies in [[Magnesium deficiency|magnesium]],<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Serefko |first1=Anna |last2=Szopa |first2=Aleksandra |last3=Poleszak |first3=Ewa |date=2016-03-01 |title=Magnesium and depression |journal=Magnesium Research |volume=29 |issue=3 |pages=112–119 |doi=10.1684/mrh.2016.0407 |doi-broken-date=11 July 2025 |issn=1952-4021 |pmid=27910808}}</ref> [[vitamin D]],<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Geng |first1=Chunmei |last2=Shaikh |first2=Abdul Sami |last3=Han |first3=Wenxiu |last4=Chen |first4=Dan |last5=Guo |first5=Yujin |last6=Jiang |first6=Pei |date=2019 |title=Vitamin D and depression: mechanisms, determination and application |journal=Asia Pacific Journal of Clinical Nutrition |volume=28 |issue=4 |pages=689–694 |doi=10.6133/apjcn.201912_28(4).0003 |issn=1440-6047 |pmid=31826364}}</ref> and [[tryptophan]] have been linked with depression; these deficiencies may be caused by the individual's environment, but they have a biological impact. Several theories concerning the biologically based cause of depression have been suggested over the years, including theories revolving around [[monoamine neurotransmitters]], [[neuroplasticity]], [[neurogenesis]], [[inflammation]] and the [[circadian rhythm]]. Physical illnesses, including [[hypothyroidism]] and [[mitochondrial disease]], can also trigger depressive symptoms.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Anglin|first1=Rebecca E.|last2=Tarnopolsky|first2=Mark A.|last3=Mazurek|first3=Michael F.|last4=Rosebush|first4=Patricia I.|date=January 2012|title=The Psychiatric Presentation of Mitochondrial Disorders in Adults|journal=The Journal of Neuropsychiatry and Clinical Neurosciences|language=en|volume=24|issue=4|pages=394–409|doi=10.1176/appi.neuropsych.11110345|pmid=23224446|issn=0895-0172}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Carroll |first=Bernard J.|date=October 2004|title=Psychoneuroendocrinology: The Scientific Basis of Clinical Practice. Edited by O. M. Wolkowitz and A. J. Rothschild. (Pp. 606; $73.95; ISBN 0-88048-857-3 pb.) American Psychiatric Publishing, Inc.: Arlington, Virginia, 2003.|url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/psychological-medicine/article/div-classtitlepsychoneuroendocrinology-the-scientific-basis-of-clinical-practice-edited-by-o-m-wolkowitz-and-a-j-rothschild-pp-606-7395-isbn-0-88048-857-3-pb-american-psychiatric-publishing-inc-arlington-virginia-2003-div/DC8C00D758EC186F3AE9A041013AD41C|journal=Psychological Medicine|language=en|volume=34|issue=7|pages=1359–1360|doi=10.1017/S0033291704213678|s2cid=73645516|issn=0033-2917|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

[[Neural circuit]]s implicated in depression include those involved in the generation and regulation of emotion, as well as in reward. Abnormalities are commonly found in the lateral prefrontal cortex whose putative function is generally considered to involve regulation of emotion. Regions involved in the generation of emotion and reward such as the [[amygdala]], [[anterior cingulate cortex|anterior cingulate cortex (ACC)]], [[orbitofrontal cortex|orbitofrontal cortex (OFC)]], and [[striatum]] are frequently implicated as well. These regions are innervated by a monoaminergic nucleus, and tentative evidence suggests a potential role for abnormal [[Monoamine neurotransmitter|monoaminergic]] activity.<ref>{{cite journal|vauthors=Kupfer DJ, Frank E, Phillips ML |title=Major depressive disorder: new clinical, neurobiological, and treatment perspectives |journal=Lancet |date=17 March 2012 |volume=379 |issue=9820 |pages=1045–55 |doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(11)60602-8 |pmid=22189047 |pmc=3397431}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=aan het Rot M, Mathew SJ, Charney DS |title=Neurobiological mechanisms in major depressive disorder |journal=Canadian Medical Association Journal |date=3 February 2009 |volume=180 |issue=3 |pages=305–13 |doi=10.1503/cmaj.080697 |pmid=19188629 |pmc=2630359}}</ref>

{{Short description|Intrinsic quantum property of particles}}
{{about|the concept in quantum mechanics|the concept in classical mechanics|Spin (geometry)}}

'''Spin''' is an [[Intrinsic and extrinsic properties|intrinsic]] form of [[angular momentum]] carried by [[elementary particle]]s, and thus by [[List of particles#Composite particles|composite particles]] such as [[hadron]]s, [[atomic nucleus|atomic nuclei]], and atoms.<ref name="merzbacher372">{{cite book |last=Merzbacher |first=Eugen |author-link=Eugen Merzbacher |title=Quantum Mechanics |url=https://archive.org/details/quantummechanics00merz_136 |url-access=limited |edition=3rd |year=1998 |pages=[https://archive.org/details/quantummechanics00merz_136/page/n385 372]–373 |publisher=John Wiley & Sons | isbn=978-0-471-88702-7 }}</ref><ref name="griffiths">{{cite book |last=Griffiths |first=David |author-link=David J. Griffiths |title=Introduction to Quantum Mechanics |url=https://archive.org/details/introductiontoqu00grif_190 |url-access=limited |edition=2nd |year=2005 }}</ref>{{rp|pp=[https://archive.org/details/introductiontoqu00grif_190/page/n194 183]–184}} Spin is [[Quantization (physics)|quantized]], and accurate models for the interaction with spin require [[relativistic quantum mechanics]] or [[quantum field theory]].

The existence of [[electron]] [[spin angular momentum]] is [[inferred]] from experiments, such as the [[Stern–Gerlach experiment]], in which silver atoms were observed to possess two possible discrete angular momenta despite having no orbital angular momentum.<ref name="eisberg272">{{cite book |last1=Eisberg |first1=Robert |last2=Resnick |first2=Robert |author-link2=Robert Resnick |title=Quantum Physics of Atoms, Molecules, Solids, Nuclei, and Particles |url=https://archive.org/details/quantumphysicsat00eisb |url-access=limited |edition=2nd |year=1985 |pages=[https://archive.org/details/quantumphysicsat00eisb/page/n288 272]–273|publisher=Wiley |isbn=978-0-471-87373-0 }}</ref> The relativistic [[spin–statistics theorem]] connects electron spin quantization to the [[Pauli exclusion principle]]: observations of exclusion imply half-integer spin, and observations of half-integer spin imply exclusion.

Spin is described mathematically as a vector for some particles such as photons, and as a [[spinor]] for other particles such as electrons. Spinors behave similarly to [[Euclidean vector|vectors]]: they have definite magnitudes and change under rotations; however, they use an unconventional "direction". All elementary particles of a given kind have the same magnitude of spin angular momentum, though its direction may change. These are indicated by assigning the particle a [[spin quantum number]].<ref name="griffiths" />{{rp|pp=[https://archive.org/details/introductiontoqu00grif_190/page/n194 183–184]}}

The [[International System of Units|SI units]] of spin are the same as classical angular momentum (i.e., [[Newton (unit)|N]]·[[metre|m]]·[[second|s]], [[Joule|J]]·s, or [[Kilogram|kg]]·m<sup>2</sup>·s<sup>−1</sup>). In quantum mechanics, angular momentum and spin angular momentum take discrete values proportional to the [[Planck constant]]. In practice, spin is usually given as a [[dimensionless]] spin quantum number by dividing the spin angular momentum by the [[reduced Planck constant]] {{mvar|ħ}}. Often, the "spin quantum number" is simply called "spin".

